  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 202 AM CST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nBy Saturday morning the old gyre covering the region early this\nmorning will have moved into the northeastern U.S., while a\ncompact but energetic 500 MB shortwave on its heels crosses \nnear the Minnesota/Canada border and another lobe deepens near \nthe Arizona/Mexico border. A weak mid-level trough stretching\nbetween these systems will move overhead will help push the\nsurface ridge lying over the region early Saturday\nsoutheastward, bringing a brief return of southerly flow to our\nforecast area. Forecast soundings also indicate the departure of\nthe ridge may finally clear the low cloud shield out, as\ndiscussed in the short term section above. Thereafter, Iowa will\nenter a west northwesterly steering flow regime in the first\nhalf of next week, generally supporting dry weather with a\nseries of rapid and weak surface troughs and ridges and steady\nairmass modification. By early next week, on any days where\nthere is sunshine and southerly flow ahead of an approaching\ntrough, temperatures may rebound well into the 60s before being\nmoderated again.",
